  "title": "Trump Says Open Borders Destroy Nations. What Happens When a Government Loses Control of Its Border?",
  "deck": "From ancient Rome to modern Lebanon, Congo and Uganda, border crises have exposed what happens when governments struggle to enforce their rules or keep up with new arrivals.",
  "category": "politics",
  "categoryLabel": "U.S. Politics",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-23T02:56:32.207Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-23T02:56:32.207Z",
  "body": [
    "President Donald Trump declared Sunday that “open borders destroy nations” and said the United States had come close to becoming one of them before he returned to office. The claim of near-collapse is sweeping. The point underneath it deserves a serious look. What happens when a government’s ability to enforce its border rules, decide who can stay and manage new arrivals falls behind events on the ground?",
    "## Rome made a decision it couldn’t manage",
    "In 376 CE, Goths fleeing the Huns gathered at the Danube, on the frontier of the Roman Empire. Emperor Valens gave one Gothic group permission to cross. Roman officials failed to provide adequate food and exploited the people they had admitted. More Goths crossed as frontier defenses weakened. The crisis escalated into armed revolt. Two years later, a Gothic force defeated the Roman army at Adrianople, killing Valens.",
    "The eastern empire survived the defeat, and the western imperial government lasted roughly another century. The immediate lesson lies in the two years between the crossing and the battle. Rome made a decision at the frontier and failed to carry it out on the ground.",
    "## Lebanon and Congo show how armed groups can carry a conflict across a border",
    "In Lebanon, hundreds of thousands of Palestinian refugees had already arrived following successive Arab-Israeli wars. After fighting with Jordan’s government in 1970, armed factions of the Palestine Liberation Organization moved into Lebanon. They launched attacks into Israel from Lebanese territory, drawing Israeli retaliation. Their presence also shifted the balance among Lebanese factions in a country already deeply divided over political power. Those tensions helped ignite Lebanon’s civil war in 1975.",
    "In Zaire, now the Democratic Republic of Congo, civilians fleeing the 1994 Rwandan genocide crossed into the country alongside former Rwandan soldiers and militia members. Armed groups regrouped in eastern Zaire and launched attacks back into Rwanda. Zaire’s president, Mobutu Sese Seko, supported the rearmament of former Rwandan troops. The cross-border fighting helped set the stage for the First Congo War and Mobutu’s overthrow. In both Lebanon and Zaire, the danger escalated when armed groups could operate from inside the host country.",
    "## Uganda shows what the strain looks like for civilians",
    "Uganda has maintained what the United Nations refugee agency calls an open-door approach to asylum. By March 2026, it hosted about two million refugees and asylum seekers, mainly from South Sudan and the Democratic Republic of Congo. The World Bank found that pressure extended beyond the settlements as nearby towns with limited budgets and staff faced rising demand for clinics, schools and other public services. Uganda shows how a national policy can remain in place while the communities carrying it out struggle to keep up.",
    "## What the American numbers tell us",
    "U.S. authorities recorded about 302,000 encounters at the southwest land border in December 2023. That fell to about 96,000 in December 2024, President Joe Biden’s last full month in office. In February 2025, Trump’s first full month back, the figure dropped to about 11,700. It was about 11,400 in October 2026. Biden-era asylum restrictions and Mexican enforcement helped drive the earlier decline. The numbers fell much further after Trump took office and have stayed comparatively low.",
    "Encounter totals measure activity recorded by border authorities. Uganda offers a closer comparison for one part of the U.S. debate because it directs attention to what happens in receiving communities. A fuller picture of U.S. capacity would also track how quickly asylum claims are decided, how consistently final removal orders are carried out, and whether communities can keep up with demand for housing and services.",
    "## What Washington does with lower crossings",
    "Two days before Trump’s post, a reporter asked whether he would veto the bipartisan Dignity Act. The proposal combines stronger border enforcement and mandatory employment verification with renewable legal status and work authorization for some undocumented immigrants who have lived in the country since before 2021. Trump defended his administration’s deportation efforts and left the veto question unanswered.",
    "The bill puts a specific choice behind Trump’s warning. Would he accept tougher enforcement at the border and in workplaces as part of a deal that also lets some longtime residents work and stay legally? The longer-term test is whether Washington can use the time it has gained from lower encounters to build a system that can enforce its decisions and handle another surge."
  ],
